 knows that it's a CSV file it wants  to see if we can build guided built now  going from that first page to here what  happened was it understood what is in  the file and this is granted a small  file but if you had a large file with  millions of rows it'll do the same thing  it understood what are the variables in  the file that are numeric and also went  in and said okay given that these are  the variables you have these are the  potential variables that you can use as  outputs in your system and that means  that for each of those outputs it will  go in and use the rest of the variables  as inputs and try and build your best  model and it not only did that it went  back and said okay here are all the  different techniques in the system  they're already pre-loaded that can be  used with this data set now remember if  you're if you're a subject-matter expert  you don't necessarily know what all of  these techniques mean you don't  necessarily need to care you just got  here the system is suggesting something  let it continue to go on up to a point  where it built some models and then you  can evaluate if the model is useful or  not that's we are going with it now  you don't necessarily agree with the all  the variables that it shows for example  if I don't want to build a model for  friction but I want to build the model  for acceleration I could do that as well  and then I go in and say okay build all  24 models now a couple of things need to  happen for this to work right where it  needs to take this data set split that  into testing and training connected to  all of these different techniques select  all the defaults and then come out with  the best models now in the time frame  that I just said that it went in built  all those models life came back and said  it's telling us what are the best models  for each kind of output that the system  told us now some of these output outputs  may not necessarily make sense for  example I don't necessarily want to  build a model for weight but I want a  model for where I want to model for  acceleration for example and it also  went in and said out of the six  techniques it went and evaluated for  each of the output it's telling you  what's the best model based on a  particular root mean square error like  it's a simple example I can annotate  scale here on the UI now what if instead  of having six variables you actually had  10,000 variables or instead of building  24 models you want to build 10,000  models or 100,000 models so we have a  CLI for that as well so let me switch to  that and to just walk you through this  one I have 10 cube contexts running on  the left-hand side and each one so the  first left-hand side ones are gke  clusters so the GK 1 2 all the way to 5  on the right hand side are our  self-managed kubernetes clusters so  that's the tower you saw so the  left-hand side is GK right hand side is  self-managed and I'm going to just run a  simple watch command so I'm going to  just watch the pods and most of it is  telling you that no resources are found  that means nothing is actually happening  there because I have not run anything  yet right now let me go in here and run  the case where I'm  actually spinning up the same thing  where we are simulating 10 different  users running a thousand models each  right so it's going to spin up 10,000  models simultaneously across all of  these different clusters so if I hit  this cluster and my fingers okay what  you're going to see is different colors  going through so each color is one  particular kubernetes cluster so it's  actually loading all of the kubernetes  cluster simultaneously and what you're  going to see is on the left hand side  each of the clusters actually starting  to to get loaded right and you can see  some of them started and it's processing  each of the nodes are going to process  about 1200 models now if I go back to  our system that's monitoring all this so  what you're seeing are all of the ten  clusters along with the management  cluster okay and what you're seeing is  the blue dots are as a management  cluster the ten kubernetes clusters are  around them and the white dots that are  popping up in are the models that are  being built live as I started building  it okay and the total number of services  that are running to make this possible  is well over a thousand services and the  jobs you can see the job count is  actually going up life so it looks like  the demo gods are five smiling on us now  we did build another case where a  hundred thousand models were running  live and I wanted to show that system as  well I'll come back and show you these  numbers and this is a case where all  100,000 models ran and completed and I  can go back to to this case reduce the  speed a little bit little bit so this is  what  a system that builds 100,000 models look  like and this was run life thank you you  can see the models are still being built  it's still being submitted and the  interesting piece here is to get to this  level of scale you can see all of them  have been submitted kubernetes is still  responding and actually kicking off the  jobs so the load balancing here not only  had to watch what is it load on the  system it also had to be optimized to  the level where it's watching which of  these clusters or which of these  kubernetes clusters are having a delay  in the responding back so sometimes the  kubernetes api takes two seconds to give  us a response back so in cases like that  we won't necessarily go load up that  particular cluster we'll move the load  to somewhere else so it's not just that  it's watching what is the what is  happening inside those clusters it's  also watching kubernetes right now you  built all these models you want to know  what is the best model so you can run a  query across all of these models that  just got built it'll come back and tell  you that it's not just one answer right  so you need to know whether it's a best  model based on root mean square error  that was what we showed on the UI but  you can also say that is the best model  based on max percentage error or average  percentage error or any of the different  percentages errors that you care about  as a data scientist or as a subject  matter expert as well right and all of  this so this is coming live from the  system that is actually running there  right and this you can see the jobs are  continuing to be submitted there you go  okay so we did show you real well world  use cases that require a lot of scale  and getting to scale requires you to  think slightly differently and also  think about scale from day one thinking  about scale at day 10 or day 100 causes  a lot more angst in thinking about it  they won right and we had to learn that  painfully on many of our applications as  well but what we showed you today was  using darker kuben arrays cube flow GCP  ng C containers in a way that is  significantly scalable and it's  definitely scalable but it takes quite a  bit of effort as well so I want to end  this with credits from the team that's  listed here and many many more  who works tirelessly for several years  to get us here so thanks a lot guys  thank you  [Music]

